{:edb/db-before-t 1381 :edb/db-after-t 1382 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534694 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:24:47.903Z" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059504 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2:= R_2 +(-4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A$?" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059504 1100 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059504 1145 "The operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-4) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 + (-4)(1) & 2 + (-4)(2) & -1 + (-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059504 1145 "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059505 1100 "Consider the augmented matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A$?" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059505 1100 "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059505 1145 "The operation\n\n$$\nR_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}R_{1}↔R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059505 1145 "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059506 1100 "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059506 1100 "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059506 1145 "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row.\n\nNow, applying the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 1 \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 + 1 \\cdot (-4) & 1 + 1 \\cdot (-2) & 2 + 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1\n$$" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059506 1145 "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059507 1100 "Given that\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059507 1100 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0$?" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059507 1145 "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0$.\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0$, because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0$.\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1$. If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22}$, we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-5) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 + (-5) \\cdot 3 & 5 + (-5) \\cdot 1 & 6 + (-5) \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 -5 R_1\n$$" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059507 1145 "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303738 13194139534694 true] [17592186059696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303745 13194139534694 true] [17592186059696 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303815 13194139534694 true] [17592186059697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303752 13194139534694 true] [17592186059697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303759 13194139534694 true] [17592186059697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303829 13194139534694 true] [17592186059697 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303836 13194139534694 true] [17592186059698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303766 13194139534694 true] [17592186059698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303773 13194139534694 true] [17592186059698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303780 13194139534694 true] [17592186059698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303787 13194139534694 true] [17592186059698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303843 13194139534694 true] [17592186059699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303794 13194139534694 true] [17592186059699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303801 13194139534694 true] [17592186059699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303808 13194139534694 true] [17592186059699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303822 13194139534694 true] [17592186059881 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534694 true] [17592186059881 1100 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$, given by\n\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1) \\cdot R_{3}$ on $M$?" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059881 1100 "Consider the augmented matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ on $M?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059881 1145 "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5+(-1)(5) & -1+(-1)(2) & 2+(-1)(-1) & 1+(-1)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059886 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534694 true] [17592186059886 1100 "Consider the matrix $M$, given by\n\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{3}:=R_{3} + 3R_{1}$ on $M$?" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059886 1100 "Consider the matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ on $M?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059886 1145 "The operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}$ means that we need to add $3$ times the first row to the third row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3+(3)(-1) & -1+(3)(2) & 9+(3)(4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059887 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534694 true] [17592186059887 1100 "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{1}↔R_{2}$ on $A$?" 13194139534694 false] [17592186059887 1100 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186059887 1145 "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186060123 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534694 true] [17592186060123 1100 "Given that\n\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes $a_{22} = 0$?" 13194139534694 false] [17592186060123 1100 "Given that\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$,\nwhich of the following elementary row operations makes ${a}_{22}=0?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186060123 1145 "Of the given options, we consider the row operation \n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the above row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3}.\n$$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186303738 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303739 13194139534694 true] [17592186303738 1089 "q-10244" 13194139534694 true] [17592186303738 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534694 true] [17592186303738 1100 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" 13194139534694 true] [17592186303738 1113 #uuid "94d28327-3100-5e77-acac-c58bb1afa861" 13194139534694 true] [175